    {
      "name": "operational_burden_flags",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Multi-label categorization of operational burdens directly answers the query, giving a rich axis to group and compare incident types. Extractions from short_description are largely sound (connectivity, access/database). However some multi-label assignments (e.g. 'server_outage|performance_degradation|email_service_burden') appear richer than terse descriptions likely support, suggesting occasional over-labeling. The 'Printing issues' row flagged 'other' with count 0 is a minor inconsistency but a reasonable fallback."
    },
    {
      "name": "operational_burden_count",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Count of distinct burden flags is a useful derived measure of incident complexity, offering a numeric axis for aggregation. It is somewhat redundant with the flags column (directly derivable), lowering leverage. Values are grounded as counts of the flags, though the 'other'=0 case is a minor definitional quirk."
    },
    {
      "name": "primary_operational_burden",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Single primary category is highly useful for clean grouping and ranking of dominant burdens, avoiding multi-label ambiguity. Values are consistent with the flags column and grounded in source text. The choice of 'primary' among multiple flags is a warranted inference, though occasionally judgment-based."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.88,
  "source_groundedness": 0.85,
  "rationale": "The three columns collectively provide strong analytical leverage for identifying and quantifying operational burdens in incident descriptions: a multi-label flag set, a complexity count, and a single primary category enabling grouping, ranking, and modeling. Values are generally well-grounded in short_description text, with categorizations that are sound readings of the source. Minor concerns include some multi-label assignments appearing more specific than terse descriptions may support, and the count column being largely derivable from the flags column. Overall the columns satisfy both necessary conditions well."
}

